  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 143 PM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nNo significant pattern changes are forecast for the next several \ndays, so the overall message may sound like a broken record when \ntalking about storm chances and hot temperatures. Another shortwave \nlooks to move across CO sometime on Thursday (which may be amplified \nby the MCV that was over AZ this morning) and help produce \nconvection along the Front Range. Scattered thunderstorms are \nlikely along the northern mountains into northeast NM for \nThursday. This feature then works to push the center of the high \npressure more to the west, which will work to shift the overall \nflow from north to south over much of NM. In turn, thunderstorms \nwill favor this storm motion, and this sets the stage for folks \nalong and just east of the central mountain chain to see a bulk of\nthe precipitation, while potentially giving a reprieve to folks \nin western NM that have seen consistent rain. Throughout every day\nthough, the hot temperatures remain with highs in the 90s to low \n100s.",
